const body = `<h1>10.1b — Recursive Patterns: Factorial, Power, and Fibonacci</h1>

<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Build: Recursive Math Methods</h3>
        <p>Yesterday you saw how a method can call itself. Today you'll write the three classic recursive functions. Each one teaches a different pattern.</p>
        <p>Create a class called <code>RecursiveMath</code>:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Factorial</strong> <code>int factorial(int n)</code>:
                <ul>
                    <li>Base case: n <= 1 → return 1</li>
                    <li>Recursive case: n * factorial(n-1)</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Power</strong> <code>int power(int base, int exp)</code>:
                <ul>
                    <li>Base case: exp == 0 → return 1</li>
                    <li>Recursive case: base * power(base, exp-1)</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Fibonacci</strong> <code>int fib(int n)</code>:
                <ul>
                    <li>Base cases: n == 0 → 0, n == 1 → 1</li>
                    <li>Recursive case: fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Watch the Performance</h3>
        <p>Try fib(40). Why is it so slow? Draw the call tree for fib(5) — how many times is fib(2) called? This is the classic recursive performance trap.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/recursion-basics.html">Recursion</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
